body:
> My compiles of 4.4 beta 2 do not have the pdb files as listed prior. So does that mean the debug output is not going to work until the fix is done?

Oh, don't worry about this file, what matters is you have the binary with debug symbols embbeded or the binary and the debug symbols file.

<br>

> Is there a particular guide or setup I should do for that? I have not done that before. I have vscode, but have not used gdb with it before.

I'll share my `launch.json` and `tasks.json` (this one only if you also wants to compile from VSCode) so you can copy-paste and create the necessary files, check the comments to see what you need to modify.

<details><summary>launch.json</summary>

```
  "configurations": [
    {
      "name": "Build And Debug With MinGW",
      "type": "cppdbg",
      "request": "launch",
	// Change this for the name of the binary you're generating, in your case without the .dev
      "program": "${workspaceFolder}/bin/godot.windows.editor.dev.x86_64.exe",
      "args": [
        "--editor",
        "--path",
	// Replace this line below with your project absolute path
        "C:/Users/Matheus/Downloads/Godot MRP/tempgodot"
      ],
      "stopAtEntry": false,
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}",
      "environment": [],
      "externalConsole": false,
      "MIMode": "gdb",
	// Replace this path with your gdb path
      "miDebuggerPath": "C:/msys64/mingw64/bin/gdb.exe",
      "visualizerFile": "${workspaceFolder}/platform/windows/godot.natvis",
      "setupCommands": [
        {
          "description": "Enable pretty-printing for gdb",
          "text": "-enable-pretty-printing",
          "ignoreFailures": true
        },
        {
          "description": "Set Disassembly Flavor to Intel",
          "text": "-gdb-set disassembly-flavor intel",
          "ignoreFailures": true
        }
      ],
	// Uncomment this line if you want to build the binary before launch, needs the tasks.json
      //"preLaunchTask": "build_with_mingw"
    },
// Below is the configs for launch and compile using MSVC, if you don't plan to use it, you can just ignore this
    {
      "name": "Build And Debug With MSVC",
      "type": "cppvsdbg",
      "request": "launch",
	// Change this for the name of the binary you're generating, in your case without the .dev
      "program": "${workspaceFolder}/bin/godot.windows.editor.dev.x86_64.exe",
      "args": [
        "--editor",
        "--path",
	// Replace this line below with your project absolute path
        "C:/Users/Matheus/Downloads/Godot MRP/tempgodot"
      ],
      "stopAtEntry": false,
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}",
      "environment": [],
      "console": "internalConsole",
      "visualizerFile": "${workspaceFolder}/platform/windows/godot.natvis",
	// Uncomment the line below if you want to compile first before launch, needs the tasks.json
      //"preLaunchTask": "build_with_msvc"
    },
  ],
  "version": "2.0.0",
}
```

</details>

<br>

<details><summary>tasks.json</summary>

```
{
    // See https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkId=733558
    // for the documentation about the tasks.json format
    "version": "2.0.0",
    "tasks": [
        {
            "label": "build_with_mingw",
            "group": "build",
            "type": "shell",
            "command": "scons",
            "args": [
		//  Same as the args you use in the cmd/powershell, modify as you need
                "use_mingw=yes",
                "lto=none",
                "dev_build=yes",
                "scu_build=yes",
                "compiledb=yes"
            ],
            "problemMatcher": "$msCompile"
        },
        {
            "label": "build_with_msvc",
            "group": "build",
            "type": "shell",
            "command": "scons",
            "args": [
		//  Same as the args you use in the cmd/powershell, modify as you need
                "dev_build=yes",
                "scu_build=yes",
                "compiledb=yes"
            ],
            "problemMatcher": "$msCompile"
        },
    ],
}
```

</details>

<br>

Create this files inside the `.vscode` directory in the same directory as the source files, open the VSCode making the directory with the source files as the workspace. so you can select `Build And Debug With MinGW` and press `F5` to launch (if you didn't uncommented the `preLaunchTask` line this will start Godot without recompile if you already compiled before)

